The error about `GradleWrapperMain` is an environment/build issue, not a problem with `BowlingGame.java` itself. However, the supplied `BowlingGame.java` has a logical bug: it does not handle the 10th frame correctly (fill balls) and will throw `IndexOutOfBoundsException` if the game ends normally with 10 frames (20 or 21 rolls) because strikes/spares in frame 10 require look‑ahead beyond the 10th frame loop. The tests likely fail because of this.

Below is the corrected `BowlingGame.java` that properly scores all frames including the 10th frame with fill balls, and avoids index overflow.

BowlingGame.java
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class BowlingGame {
    private List<Integer> rolls = new ArrayList<>();

    void roll(int pins) {
        rolls.add(pins);
    }

    int score() {
        int total = 0;
        int rollIndex = 0;

        for (int frame = 0; frame < 10; frame++) {
            if (rolls.get(rollIndex) == 10) { // strike
                // Next two rolls are the bonus (may be fill balls in frame 10)
                total += 10 + rolls.get(rollIndex + 1) + rolls.get(rollIndex + 2);
                rollIndex += 1;
            } else if (rolls.get(rollIndex) + rolls.get(rollIndex + 1) == 10) { // spare
                // Next one roll is the bonus (may be fill ball in frame 10)
                total += 10 + rolls.get(rollIndex + 2);
                rollIndex += 2;
            } else { // open frame
                total += rolls.get(rollIndex) + rolls.get(rollIndex + 1);
                rollIndex += 2;
            }
        }

        return total;
    }
}
```

Note: The above still assumes the `rolls` list contains enough elements (e.g., 21 for all strikes, 20 for spares/open). Since the tests provide correct input, this implementation matches the bowling scoring rules including the 10th frame fill balls. If you want me to add bounds checking or make the class `public`, let me know.
