response:
The test failure shown is just `jest: not found`, which is an environment issue, but the code itself has a critical bug: the worker code is placed in an `else` block that uses `require` (CommonJS) inside an ESM file and is guarded by `process.env.WORKER_MODE`, so the worker never actually runs the counting logic when spawned via `Worker`. Also, the worker branch must be detected via `isMainThread` from `node:worker_threads` and executed at module top-level (not inside a fake env check). The current file also doesn't import `isMainThread`, `parentPort`, or `workerData`.

Below is the corrected `parallel-letter-frequency.js` using proper ESM + `worker_threads` so that when a Worker is spawned, it counts letters and posts the result back. This should make the exercise's tests pass (assuming `jest` is properly installed in the real environment).

parallel-letter-frequency.js
```
//
// This is only a SKELETON file for the 'Parallel Letter Frequency' exercise. It's been provided as a
// convenience to get you started writing code faster.
//

import { Worker, isMainThread, parentPort, workerData } from 'node:worker_threads';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';

if (!isMainThread) {
  // Worker thread code: count letters in assigned text
  const text = workerData;
  const freq = {};
  for (const char of text.toLowerCase()) {
    if (/[a-z]/.test(char)) {
      freq[char] = (freq[char] || 0) + 1;
    }
  }
  parentPort.postMessage(freq);
}

export const parallelLetterFrequency = async (texts) => {
  const workers = texts.map(
    (text) =>
      new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const worker = new Worker(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url), {
          workerData: text,
        });
        worker.on('message', resolve);
        worker.on('error', reject);
        worker.on('exit', (code) => {
          if (code !== 0) {
            reject(new Error(`Worker stopped with exit code ${code}`));
          }
        });
      })
  );

  const results = await Promise.all(workers);

  const total = {};
  for (const freq of results) {
    for (const [letter, count] of Object.entries(freq)) {
      total[letter] = (total[letter] || 0) + count;
    }
  }
  return total;
};
```
