I will refactor `BuildTree.java` to be more efficient and cleaner while keeping the same class/method names and behavior.

Key improvements:
- Remove the nested O(n²) loops for matching records and nodes by using a `HashMap<Integer, TreeNode>` for O(1) lookups.
- Simplify validation logic and avoid redundant scans.
- Build children in a single pass after creating all nodes.
- Keep throwing `InvalidRecordsException` for the same invalid conditions.

BuildTree.java
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class BuildTree {

    TreeNode buildTree(ArrayList<Record> records) throws InvalidRecordsException {
        if (records == null || records.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }

        // Sort records by record ID for ordered processing and validation
        records.sort(Comparator.comparing(Record::getRecordId));

        // Validate contiguous IDs from 0 to records.size()-1 and root condition
        for (int i = 0; i < records.size(); i++) {
            Record record = records.get(i);
            if (record.getRecordId() != i) {
                throw new InvalidRecordsException("Invalid Records");
            }
            if (record.getRecordId() == 0) {
                if (record.getParentId() != 0) {
                    throw new InvalidRecordsException("Invalid Records");
                }
            } else {
                if (record.getParentId() >= record.getRecordId()) {
                    throw new InvalidRecordsException("Invalid Records");
                }
            }
        }

        // Create all tree nodes and map them by ID
        Map<Integer, TreeNode> nodeMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (Record record : records) {
            nodeMap.put(record.getRecordId(), new TreeNode(record.getRecordId()));
        }

        // Assign children using the map for O(1) access
        for (Record record : records) {
            if (record.getRecordId() != 0) {
                TreeNode parent = nodeMap.get(record.getParentId());
                TreeNode child = nodeMap.get(record.getRecordId());
                parent.getChildren().add(child);
            }
        }

        return nodeMap.get(0);
    }

}
```

Note: This refactored version assumes `TreeNode` has a `getChildren()` method returning a mutable list (as used in the original code) and that `InvalidRecordsException` is defined elsewhere in the project. The logic preserves all original validation rules and tree structure.
