The error you are seeing (`Could not find or load main class org.gradle.wrapper.GradleWrapperMain`) is related to the Gradle wrapper, not to the source code in `ChangeCalculator.java`. However, since you asked to fix the code in `ChangeCalculator.java` to resolve the errors and the tests are correct, I reviewed the class for correctness against the instructions and edge cases.

The current implementation uses a greedy algorithm (sorting coins descending and taking the largest first). This works for canonical coin systems like `[1, 5, 10, 25, 100]` but fails for arbitrary coin sets (e.g., `[1, 3, 4]` with amount 6 should return `[3, 3]` not `[4, 1]`). The instructions explicitly ask: "Does your algorithm work for any given set of coins?" The greedy approach does not. To be correct for *any* given set of coins, we must use dynamic programming (or backtracking) to find the fewest coins.

Below is the updated `ChangeCalculator.java` using a dynamic programming approach that works for any coin set, handles negative amounts, amounts smaller than the smallest coin, and returns the fewest coins.

ChangeCalculator.java
```
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;

class ChangeCalculator {
    private List<Integer> currencyCoins;

    ChangeCalculator(List<Integer> currencyCoins) {
        // Defensive copy and sort ascending for DP indexing
        this.currencyCoins = new ArrayList<>(currencyCoins);
        Collections.sort(this.currencyCoins);
    }

    List<Integer> computeMostEfficientChange(int grandTotal) {
        if (grandTotal < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot compute change for a negative amount");
        }
        if (grandTotal == 0) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        int smallest = currencyCoins.get(0);
        if (grandTotal < smallest) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot make change for amount smaller than smallest coin");
        }

        // DP array: minCoins[i] = fewest coins to make amount i
        int[] minCoins = new int[grandTotal + 1];
        // coinUsed[i] = last coin used to make amount i
        int[] coinUsed = new int[grandTotal + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= grandTotal; i++) {
            minCoins[i] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        }

        for (int coin : currencyCoins) {
            if (coin <= grandTotal) {
                minCoins[coin] = 1;
                coinUsed[coin] = coin;
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= grandTotal; i++) {
            if (minCoins[i] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                for (int coin : currencyCoins) {
                    if (coin <= i && minCoins[i - coin] != Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                        int candidate = minCoins[i - coin] + 1;
                        if (candidate < minCoins[i]) {
                            minCoins[i] = candidate;
                            coinUsed[i] = coin;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        if (minCoins[grandTotal] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot make exact change with the given coins");
        }

        // Reconstruct the coins used
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int remaining = grandTotal;
        while (remaining > 0) {
            int coin = coinUsed[remaining];
            result.add(coin);
            remaining -= coin;
        }

        Collections.sort(result);
        return result;
    }
}
```

This change ensures the algorithm works for any given set of coins (not just canonical ones), properly handles the edge cases described, and keeps the existing class and method names intact. The Gradle wrapper error is environmental and not caused by this source file.
