I'll implement the `combinations` function according to the Killer Sudoku Helper instructions.

The function needs to:
1. Take a `cage` object with properties like `sum` (target sum), `size` (number of cells), and optionally `exclude` (array of digits already present in related row/column/box).
2. Generate all combinations of `size` distinct digits from 1-9 that add up to `sum` and don't include any excluded digits.
3. Return sorted combinations (each combination sorted ascending, and the list sorted lexicographically).

killer-sudoku-helper.js
```
//
// This is only a SKELETON file for the 'Killer Sudoku Helper' exercise. It's been provided as a
// convenience to get you started writing code faster.
//

export const combinations = (cage) => {
  const { sum, size, exclude = [] } = cage;
  const excludeSet = new Set(exclude);
  const result = [];

  const digits = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9].filter(d => !excludeSet.has(d));

  const backtrack = (start, current, currentSum) => {
    if (current.length === size) {
      if (currentSum === sum) {
        result.push([...current]);
      }
      return;
    }
    for (let i = start; i < digits.length; i++) {
      const digit = digits[i];
      backtrack(i + 1, [...current, digit], currentSum + digit);
    }
  };

  backtrack(0, [], 0);

  // Sort combinations lexicographically
  result.sort((a, b) => {
    for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
      if (a[i] !== b[i]) return a[i] - b[i];
    }
    return 0;
  });

  return result;
};
```
