The error about `org.gradle.wrapper.GradleWrapperMain` is unrelated to the Java source code logic itself — it indicates the Gradle wrapper is missing or misconfigured in the project environment, not a problem in `BookStore.java`. However, since you asked to fix the code in `BookStore.java` to resolve the errors and the tests are correct, I reviewed the implementation for correctness against the described discount rules.

The current recursive algorithm is actually a valid approach to find the optimal grouping discount. But there is one subtle bug: when `groupSize` is greater than the number of available distinct books (`maxGroupSize`), the loop already limits to `maxGroupSize`, which is fine. However, the discount array indexing uses `groupSize` directly; for `groupSize == 1`, `DISCOUNTS[1]` is `0.0` (no discount), which is correct. The algorithm explores all groupings and picks the minimum cost.

One improvement to ensure robustness and avoid any potential issues with empty lists or sorting: the code is already safe. But to be absolutely sure the class is usable as a library (no main method needed) and matches typical unit-test expectations (e.g., from a Gradle Java project), we should ensure the class is `public` so it can be accessed by test classes in different packages. Currently it is package-private. Making it `public` can help avoid class loading issues in some test setups.

Below is the updated `BookStore.java` with the class declared `public` (and no other logic changes, since the algorithm is correct for the given problem). This should not break any existing test references.

BookStore.java
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.HashMap;

public class BookStore {

    private static final double BOOK_PRICE = 8.0;
    private static final double[] DISCOUNTS = {0.0, 0.0, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.25};

    public double calculateBasketCost(List<Integer> books) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
        for (Integer book : books) {
            counts.put(book, counts.getOrDefault(book, 0) + 1);
        }

        List<Integer> sortedCounts = new ArrayList<>(counts.values());
        sortedCounts.sort((a, b) -> b - a); // descending

        return calculateOptimal(sortedCounts);
    }

    private double calculateOptimal(List<Integer> sortedCounts) {
        if (sortedCounts.isEmpty()) {
            return 0.0;
        }

        double best = Double.MAX_VALUE;
        // Try all possible group sizes from 1 to 5 (number of distinct books available)
        int maxGroupSize = sortedCounts.size();
        for (int groupSize = 1; groupSize <= maxGroupSize; groupSize++) {
            // Make a working copy of counts
            List<Integer> work = new ArrayList<>(sortedCounts);
            // Remove one book from each of the top 'groupSize' categories
            List<Integer> reduced = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < groupSize; i++) {
                int c = work.get(i) - 1;
                if (c > 0) {
                    reduced.add(c);
                }
            }
            // Add remaining counts (those beyond groupSize)
            for (int i = groupSize; i < work.size(); i++) {
                reduced.add(work.get(i));
            }
            reduced.sort((a, b) -> b - a);

            double costForGroup = groupSize * BOOK_PRICE * (1 - DISCOUNTS[groupSize]);
            double total = costForGroup + calculateOptimal(reduced);
            if (total < best) {
                best = total;
            }
        }
        return best;
    }

}
```
