I will implement the Robot Name exercise according to the instructions.

Changes needed:
1. Add a static set to track used names (for uniqueness).
2. Implement a method to generate a random name (two uppercase letters + three digits).
3. Give `Robot` a `name` getter that lazily assigns a unique random name on first access, and a `reset` method that clears the current name so a new one is generated next time.
4. Implement `Robot.releaseNames` to clear all used names (as implied by the skeleton).

robot-name.js
```
// This is only a SKELETON file for the 'Robot Name' exercise. It's been
// provided as a convenience to get your started writing code faster.

const usedNames = new Set();

function generateName() {
  const letters = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ';
  const digits = '0123456789';
  let name;
  do {
    let candidate = '';
    for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
      candidate += letters.charAt(Math.floor(Math.random() * letters.length));
    }
    for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
      candidate += digits.charAt(Math.floor(Math.random() * digits.length));
    }
    name = candidate;
  } while (usedNames.has(name));
  usedNames.add(name);
  return name;
}

export class Robot {
  constructor() {
    this._name = null;
  }

  get name() {
    if (this._name === null) {
      this._name = generateName();
    }
    return this._name;
  }

  reset() {
    if (this._name !== null) {
      usedNames.delete(this._name);
      this._name = null;
    }
  }
}

Robot.releaseNames = () => {
  usedNames.clear();
};
```
