The error shown is `jest: not found`, which means the test runner isn't installed or available in the environment. However, the instructions say the tests are correct and not to change them, and to fix the code in `robot-name.js` to resolve the errors.

Looking at the provided `robot-name.js`, it uses ES module syntax (`export class Robot`). If the test environment expects CommonJS (which Jest often does by default in typical Node setups without ESM config), the `export` keyword will cause a syntax/parse error when Jest tries to load the module, leading to failures that may surface as the suite not running. To make the code compatible with standard Jest/CommonJS without changing the public API or function/class names, we should export the `Robot` class using `module.exports` (or `exports`) instead of the `export` keyword.

Additionally, the `Robot.releaseNames` static method is assigned after the class; that's fine, but we must ensure it's exported too if needed. We'll export the class and attach the static method in a CJS-friendly way.

Here is the updated file using CommonJS exports so it can be required by Jest without ESM issues:

robot-name.js
```
// This is only a SKELETON file for the 'Robot Name' exercise. It's been
// provided as a convenience to get your started writing code faster.

const usedNames = new Set();

function generateName() {
  const letters = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ';
  const digits = '0123456789';
  let name;
  do {
    let candidate = '';
    for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
      candidate += letters.charAt(Math.floor(Math.random() * letters.length));
    }
    for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
      candidate += digits.charAt(Math.floor(Math.random() * digits.length));
    }
    name = candidate;
  } while (usedNames.has(name));
  usedNames.add(name);
  return name;
}

class Robot {
  constructor() {
    this._name = null;
  }

  get name() {
    if (this._name === null) {
      this._name = generateName();
    }
    return this._name;
  }

  reset() {
    if (this._name !== null) {
      usedNames.delete(this._name);
      this._name = null;
    }
  }
}

Robot.releaseNames = () => {
  usedNames.clear();
};

module.exports = Robot;
```

This keeps the same behavior and API (`Robot`, `name` getter, `reset`, `Robot.releaseNames`) while making it loadable in a standard Jest/CommonJS environment.
