The test code calls `gigasecond::advance(time_from_string(...))`, where `time_from_string` returns a `boost::posix_time::ptime` (from Boost.Date_Time). The tests expect a function named `advance` (not `gigasecond_after`) that 
takes a `boost::posix_time::ptime` and returns the time one gigasecond later, also as a `boost::posix_time::ptime`. The project already links Boost.Date_Time. We must change the header and implementation to provide 
`gigasecond::advance` using Boost types, while keeping the namespace `gigasecond`. We should not change the test file.



We'll update `gigasecond.h` to include `<boost/date_time/posix_time/posix_time.hpp>` and declare:

`boost::posix_time::ptime advance(boost::posix_time::ptime from);`



In `gigasecond.cpp`, implement by converting the ptime to a time_t (or using a boost::posix_time::time_duration of 1,000,000,000 seconds). Boost provides `boost::posix_time::seconds(1000000000)`. We can just add that duration 
to the ptime. That's clean and uses only the linked Boost library (which is allowed as it's already found by CMake). The instructions said "only use standard libraries, don't suggest installing any packages" but the build 
already uses Boost date_time, so using it is fine. Alternatively we could keep standard, but the test uses `time_from_string` which is Boost. So we must match.



Thus we replace the existing `gigasecond_after` with `advance`. We'll keep the header guard.



